---
/**
 * OpenSplitSection — reusable open-layout content | image SPLIT composition.
 *
 * Identity: ONE editorial two-column composition on an OPEN page background.
 * No enclosing card shell, no boxed panel, no border, no shadow, no decorative
 * split divider (DESIGN.md §6 "No cardification"; design-foundation-governance.md
 * §6/§8). The image feels integrated into the page architecture, not pinned
 * inside a container. Content stays left-aligned; the image uses the approved
 * card/media radius, never the control or section radius (task §9).
 *
 * OWNERSHIP (task §2): the component owns the COMPOSITION ONLY — the
 * responsive two-column grid, the desktop column ratio, the desktop visual
 * direction, the mobile ordering rule, the internal column gap, and the image
 * media frame. It does NOT own the page <Section>, <PageContainer>, section
 * spacing, surface, or neighboring section rhythm — those belong to the caller
 * so the page blueprint controls section rhythm and composes the split cleanly.
 * The caller composes:
 *
 *   <Section surface="open-dark" spacing aria-labelledby>
 *     <PageContainer>
 *       <OpenSplitSection image={…} imageAlt={…} layout ratio mobileOrder>
 *         <div slot="content">…SectionHeading / <p> / <Link>…</div>
 *       </OpenSplitSection>
 *     </PageContainer>
 *   </Section>
 *
 * It owns NO page copy: the heading, body, CTAs, lists, and page-specific
 * semantics arrive through the named `content` slot (component-architecture.md
 * §3/§18; task §3). It does NOT add props for heading/description/buttons/
 * features/eyebrow — the slot exists precisely to avoid that. It does NOT style
 * slotted h2/p/a/ul via descendant selectors; the caller composes
 * SectionHeading / <p> / <Link> (task §10 — no local typography overrides; the
 * active theme owns the heading family).
 *
 * Ratios describe the visual LEFT / RIGHT columns (task §5):
 *
 *   ratio="5-7" + layout="content-image" → content 5 (left) / image 7 (right)
 *   ratio="5-7" + layout="image-content" → image 5 (left) / content 7 (right)
 *
 * Only the three locked desktop ratios exist (responsive-layout.md §2;
 * design-foundation-governance.md §13): 5/7, 6/6, 7/5. No other proportions.
 *
 * Desktop direction + row placement (task §1/§5/§6 — HARD REQUIREMENT): the DOM
 * is ALWAYS content → image so the reading order is correct by default. At the
 * desktop breakpoint the two columns are placed EXPLICITLY via CSS grid
 * `grid-column` (col-start / col-span) AND `grid-row: 1` (row-start) — every
 * placement combination pins BOTH grid children to desktop row 1. Explicit row
 * placement is authoritative: it defeats CSS-grid sparse auto-placement, which
 * is exactly the reverse-layout bug (the late-in-row-1 first item advances the
 * auto-placement cursor past row 1, so the second item falls to row 2 instead
 * of backfilling the empty columns). Pinning row 1 on both children guarantees a
 * single-row split for all six ratio/direction combinations. Explicit
 * `grid-column` also overrides `order`, so `layout="image-content"` puts the
 * image visually left WITHOUT reordering the DOM — mobile therefore stays
 * content-first for free.
 *
 * Mobile order (task §4/§8): default `content-first` (content above image) at
 * all widths, including when the desktop direction is `image-content`.
 * `mobileOrder="image-first"` is an explicit blueprint exception only; it is
 * achieved with CSS `order` at the base (mobile) tier so the DOM/reading order
 * stays content → image — the documented trade-off is that a sighted user sees
 * the image first while a screen reader reads content first. At desktop,
 * explicit grid placement overrides `order`, so the image-first exception only
 * changes the MOBILE stack, never the desktop direction (always `layout`). It is
 * never the default.
 *
 * Responsive breakpoint strategy (task §21; responsive-layout.md §5/§6):
 * mobile and tablet portrait (below `lg` / 1024px) → single column; tablet
 * landscape + desktop (`lg`+) → the 12-column split. 5/7 would be cramped at
 * tablet portrait (~768px), so the split activates only at `lg`. This uses the
 * project's existing breakpoint vocabulary (Tailwind `lg`) — no new breakpoints
 * invented (responsive-ui.md fixed set 320/768/1024/1440/1920).
 *
 * Column gap (task §6): a restrained internal gap (not a section-rhythm token)
 * so the two columns read as ONE composition, not two floating blocks. The gap
 * uses the internal spacing scale (design-foundation-governance.md §11), never
 * --space-section-* (those are page-level rhythm, owned by the caller's
 * <Section>). Vertical alignment is `align-items: center` for a calm split — no
 * internal top/bottom padding (task §7; section padding belongs to the parent).
 *
 * Content measure (task §8): the content wrapper is NOT globally capped to a
 * narrow reading measure — it uses the available column width so a 7-column
 * content area is not forced to read like a 4-column text block. Paragraphs /
 * descriptions are capped by the caller through the normal SectionHeading /
 * reading-measure primitives, not by this wrapper.
 *
 * Image (task §9): an imported ImageMetadata asset rendered through
 * astro:assets <Image> (the approved pipeline — FND-IMG-08 only bans raw
 * <img>/<picture>; the foundation Image primitive types `src: string` and
 * cannot accept an imported ImageMetadata, so astro:assets <Image> is used
 * directly, mirroring ServiceCard / FinalCTA). The media frame is 4:3 /
 * object-cover — the locked split-contextual image role (imagery-art-direction.md
 * §1/§2/§3). Lazy by default (this is explanatory content, not the LCP) and
 * reserved 4:3 space (no CLS). `--radius-card` (12px) — the media/card radius,
 * never --radius-control or --radius-section. No card shell, border, shadow, or
 * nested panel. No focal-point API is exposed (object-position: center default);
 * focal positioning is a future real-asset integration concern, not an arbitrary
 * CSS-string prop.
 *
 * Alt contract: `imageAlt: ""` → decorative (alt="" + role="presentation");
 * non-empty → informative image with that alt. The heading/content are never
 * copied into alt (imagery-art-direction.md §19).
 *
 * Motion: NONE. No IntersectionObserver, no parallax, no image zoom, no
 * scale/translate hover, no slide-in, no client island. The page may later own
 * approved reveal behavior; OpenSplitSection is structural only
 * (component-architecture.md §21).
 *
 * Accessibility: DOM order supports reading order; alt semantics correct; no
 * content hidden at any breakpoint; no interaction added by the layout
 * component; the desktop visual reorder (image-content) is CSS-grid placement
 * only and does not confuse keyboard/reading order. Because the component no
 * longer owns <Section>, it does not take a `labelledBy` — the caller's
 * <Section> owns the landmark + `aria-labelledby` (FND-A11Y landmarks).
 *
 * CSS logical properties only (FND-I18N-13). class passthrough is layout-only
 * (FND-UI-06).
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import type { OpenSplitSectionProps } from "./OpenSplitSection.types.ts";

type Props = OpenSplitSectionProps;

const {
  image,
  imageAlt,
  layout = "content-image",
  ratio = "6-6",
  mobileOrder = "content-first",
  mediaHeight = "ratio",
  mediaBleed = false,
  mediaOverlay = false,
  class: className,
} = Astro.props;

// --- Desktop column placement -------------------------------------------------
// 12-column grid: explicit col-start / col-span AND row-start 1 for EACH cell,
// keyed by `${layout}--${ratio}`. Ratio = visual LEFT / RIGHT columns (task §5),
// so for `image-content` the image takes the LEFT columns and content the RIGHT.
// These are complete static class strings (tailwind-v4.md §12/§13) — never
// constructed dynamically. Explicit row-start 1 on BOTH children pins them to
// desktop row 1, defeating sparse auto-placement so the reverse (image-content)
// direction never collapses to two rows (task §1). Explicit grid-column
// placement overrides `order`, which is what lets `layout="image-content"`
// reverse the VISUAL order without touching DOM/reading order (task §6/§8).
type CellPlacement = { content: string; image: string };

const PLACEMENT: Record<string, CellPlacement> = {
  // content-image: content = LEFT columns, image = RIGHT columns
  "content-image--5-7": {
    content: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-1 lg:col-span-5",
    image: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-6 lg:col-span-7",
  },
  "content-image--6-6": {
    content: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-1 lg:col-span-6",
    image: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-7 lg:col-span-6",
  },
  "content-image--7-5": {
    content: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-1 lg:col-span-7",
    image: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-8 lg:col-span-5",
  },
  // image-content: image = LEFT columns, content = RIGHT columns
  "image-content--5-7": {
    content: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-6 lg:col-span-7",
    image: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-1 lg:col-span-5",
  },
  "image-content--6-6": {
    content: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-7 lg:col-span-6",
    image: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-1 lg:col-span-6",
  },
  "image-content--7-5": {
    content: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-8 lg:col-span-5",
    image: "lg:row-start-1 lg:col-start-1 lg:col-span-7",
  },
};

const placement =
  PLACEMENT[`${layout}--${ratio}`] ?? PLACEMENT["content-image--6-6"];

// --- Mobile order -------------------------------------------------------------
// Default `content-first`: no order classes — DOM is content → image, so the
// mobile single-column stack is already content-first. `image-first` is the
// explicit exception: CSS `order` at the base (mobile) tier visually moves the
// image above the content WITHOUT changing DOM/reading order. At `lg` the
// explicit grid-column placement overrides `order`, so this exception only
// affects the mobile stack, never the desktop direction (task §4/§8).
const mobileOrderClass =
  mobileOrder === "image-first"
    ? { content: "order-2", image: "order-1" }
    : { content: "", image: "" };

// --- Alt contract -------------------------------------------------------------
// Empty alt = decorative (alt="" + role="presentation") — the common case for
// an atmospheric split photo whose meaning is carried by the slotted content.
// Non-empty = informative image with that alt. The heading is never copied
// into alt (imagery-art-direction.md §19; task §9).
const isDecorative = imageAlt === "";
---

<div class:list={[
  "open-split",
  mediaBleed && "open-split--media-bleed",
  mediaHeight === "match-content" && "open-split--match-content",
  className,
]}>
  <div class:list={["open-split__content", placement.content, mobileOrderClass.content]}>
    <slot name="content" />
  </div>

  <figure class:list={["open-split__media", placement.image, mobileOrderClass.image]}>
    <Image
      class="open-split__image"
      src={image}
      alt={imageAlt}
      role={isDecorative ? "presentation" : undefined}
      loading="lazy"
      widths={[400, 640, 800, 1000]}
      sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 42rem, (min-width: 48rem) 38rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
    />
    {mediaOverlay && <div class="open-split__overlay" aria-hidden="true" />}
  </figure>
</div>

<style>
  /* --- Grid: mobile single column → desktop 12-col split at lg (1024px) ------- */
  /* `lg` = tablet-landscape/desktop activation point. Below lg the composition
     is a single column (mobile + tablet portrait) so 5/7 never renders cramped
     at ~768px (responsive-layout.md §5/§6). The gap uses the internal spacing
     scale, NOT section-rhythm tokens (design-foundation-governance.md §11) —
     --space-8 keeps the two columns connected as one composition at desktop,
     never a feature-tier gap that floats them apart (task §6). align-items:
     center calms the two columns vertically without stretching short content to
     the 4:3 image height (task §7). No internal top/bottom padding — section
     rhythm belongs to the caller's <Section>. */
  .open-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    align-items: center;
  }
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .open-split {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-8);
      align-items: center;
    }

    .open-split--match-content {
      align-items: stretch;
    }

    .open-split--match-content .open-split__content {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      justify-content: center;
    }

    .open-split--match-content .open-split__media {
      align-self: stretch;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
    }
  }

  /* --- Content cell --------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Structural width only — NOT a card (no bg/border/shadow/radius). min-w-0
     prevents grid overflow from long unbreakable slotted content. The content
     wrapper is NOT capped to a narrow reading measure — it uses the available
     column width so a 7-column content area is not forced to read like a
     4-column block; the caller caps individual paragraphs/descriptions via
     SectionHeading / reading-measure primitives (task §8). Left-aligned
     (inherited). */
  .open-split__content {
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  /* --- Media frame: the 4:3 contextual split image (imagery-art-direction.md
     §1/§2/§3). --radius-card (12px) — the media/card radius, never the control
     or section radius (task §9). overflow:hidden clips the object-cover image to
     the rounded frame. A neutral elevated surface shows through while the image
     loads (no broken-tile flash), mirroring ServiceCard. No card shell, border,
     or shadow — the frame is open. The frame owns the 4:3 ratio so the image
     role is guaranteed regardless of source-asset dimensions. The <figure> is a
     semantic grouping container for the standalone split media (no figcaption
     — the slotted content carries the meaning). */
  .open-split__media {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    inline-size: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    background: var(--color-surface-elevated);
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* Full-bleed image: fills the media frame edge to edge, object-cover preserves
     the 4:3 crop without distortion. object-position: center default — no
     focal-point API is exposed; focal positioning is a future real-asset
     integration concern. z-index:0 anchors it below any future overlay.
     No hover effect — no zoom/scale/filter (no motion). */
  .open-split__image {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    z-index: 0;
  }

  /* Full-height panel-side media is opt-in. The negative edges consume the
     active feature rhythm and page gutter so the image reaches the containing
     panel's top, bottom, and inline-end edge. */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .open-split--media-bleed {
      margin-block: calc(-1 * var(--space-section-feature));
      margin-inline-end: calc(-1 * var(--gutter-page));
    }

    .open-split--media-bleed .open-split__media {
      align-self: stretch;
      block-size: 100%;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
      border-start-end-radius: 0;
      border-end-end-radius: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Same bottom-weighted semantic scrim used by ServiceCard. */
  .open-split__overlay {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      var(--color-background) 0%,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-background), transparent 30%) 45%,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-background), transparent 70%) 72%,
      transparent 100%
    );
  }

  /* --- Reduced motion: nothing to disable — there is no motion. The rule is
     intentionally empty but present to document the contract. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .open-split__image {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
